= Solution
{parent=1}

Set
$$
h=\frac{x+x^*}{2},\qquad
k=\frac{x-x^*}{2i}.
$$
Both are hermitian and $x=h+ik$. If also $x=h'+ik'$ with hermitian $h',k'$, taking adjoints and then adding or subtracting the two equations gives $h=h'$ and $k=k'$.

= 2
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

For hermitian $x$ with $\|x\|\leq1$, its spectrum lies in $[-1,1]$. Continuous functional calculus therefore shows that $1-x^2$ is positive. Let
$$
v=(1-x^2)^{1/2}.
$$
It commutes with $x$, and
$$
u=x+iv
$$
satisfies $u^*u=uu^*=x^2+v^2=1$. Thus $u$ is <unitary operator>[unitary] and
$$
x=\frac12(u+u^*).
$$

